    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>The best Houston suburbs for families usually include Katy, Sugar Land, The Woodlands, Pearland, Cypress, Friendswood, League City, Kingwood and the Tomball and Fulshear corridor. Each offers established public school districts, plenty of parks and newer housing, but they differ sharply on commute, flood history and total tax rate.</p>
<p>Below is a practical look at each area: what it feels like, which school districts serve it, how the commute works and what to check before you buy. We have avoided made-up rankings and statistics; verify school ratings and tax rates for the exact address you are considering.</p>

<h2>How to Compare Houston Suburbs</h2>
<p>Before falling for a model home, set your filters. Houston is huge, and the wrong suburb can mean an hour each way in traffic.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Commute:</strong> Map your drive at rush hour, not on a Sunday. Note which freeway or toll road you will depend on.</li>
<li><strong>School district and campus:</strong> Districts are large and city limits do not match district lines. Confirm the zoned schools for the address with the district's own lookup tool.</li>
<li><strong>Total tax rate:</strong> Add county, city, school, MUD and any other special district rates. Our <a href="/tools/texas-property-tax-calculator/">Texas property tax calculator</a> helps you compare.</li>
<li><strong>Flood history:</strong> Check the FEMA map, the seller's disclosure and what happened in past storms on that street.</li>
<li><strong>HOA rules and fees:</strong> Master-planned communities often have amenities, deed restrictions and fees that vary widely.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Katy</h2>
<p><strong>Vibe:</strong> Fast-growing and family-centered, with large master-planned communities, sports complexes and a strong local identity around Katy ISD.</p>
<p><strong>Schools:</strong> Katy ISD serves most of the area. Some fringe neighborhoods fall into neighboring districts, so confirm the zoning.</p>
<p><strong>Commute:</strong> I-10 (the Katy Freeway) runs straight to the Energy Corridor and downtown. The Grand Parkway (SH 99) and Westpark Tollway help with north-south and alternate routes.</p>
<p><strong>Flood considerations:</strong> The eastern side of the Katy area sits near the Addicks and Barker reservoirs. During Hurricane Harvey in 2017, thousands of homes in the reservoir areas flooded. Ask whether a property is inside or near a reservoir's flood pool and look up its history.</p>
<p><strong>What to check:</strong> MUD tax rates, which side of I-10 the home is on, and how long the commute really takes from newer far-west sections.</p>

<h2>Sugar Land</h2>
<p><strong>Vibe:</strong> A mature, established city in Fort Bend County with a polished town center, diverse population and many neighborhoods built around lakes and golf courses.</p>
<p><strong>Schools:</strong> Fort Bend ISD serves most of Sugar Land. Some areas, such as the Greatwood community, are zoned to Lamar CISD.</p>
<p><strong>Commute:</strong> US-59/I-69 (the Southwest Freeway) and the Fort Bend Toll Road connect to the Galleria, downtown and the Texas Medical Center.</p>
<p><strong>Flood considerations:</strong> Many neighborhoods sit behind levees maintained by levee improvement districts along the Brazos River. The City of Sugar Land says levee-protected areas are not classified as floodplain, but that protection holds only while the river stays below the top of the levee and the levee is not breached.</p>
<p><strong>What to check:</strong> Whether the home is in a levee improvement district, its tax rate, and the age of the home's roof and HVAC in older sections.</p>

<h2>The Woodlands</h2>
<p><strong>Vibe:</strong> A master-planned community north of Houston, known for its forested setting, trails, villages and a busy town center. It is governed by The Woodlands Township rather than a city.</p>
<p><strong>Schools:</strong> According to The Woodlands Township, children may attend Conroe ISD, Tomball ISD or Magnolia ISD depending on location. Conroe ISD serves most of the community.</p>
<p><strong>Commute:</strong> I-45 and the Hardy Toll Road run south toward downtown and Bush Intercontinental Airport. Many residents work locally or in the north Houston job centers, which shortens commutes.</p>
<p><strong>Flood considerations:</strong> Spring Creek and other waterways run through and along the community. Check the exact parcel rather than assuming a wooded area is high and dry.</p>
<p><strong>What to check:</strong> Which village and school district the home falls in, township and HOA assessments, and the commute if you work inside the Loop.</p>

<h2>Pearland</h2>
<p><strong>Vibe:</strong> A growing city just south of Houston, with a mix of established neighborhoods and newer master-planned areas like Shadow Creek Ranch, plus easy access to the Texas Medical Center.</p>
<p><strong>Schools:</strong> Pearland ISD serves most of the city. Parts of Pearland are zoned to Alvin ISD, and smaller areas fall into other districts, including Fort Bend ISD.</p>
<p><strong>Commute:</strong> SH 288, including its toll lanes, is the main route north to the Medical Center and downtown. Beltway 8 helps for east-west trips.</p>
<p><strong>Flood considerations:</strong> Clear Creek and its tributaries run through the area. Low-lying streets near creeks deserve extra scrutiny.</p>
<p><strong>What to check:</strong> School district for the specific address, MUD and city tax rates, and drainage history on the street.</p>

<h2>Cypress</h2>
<p><strong>Vibe:</strong> A large, unincorporated area of northwest Harris County with many subdivisions and master-planned communities such as Bridgeland and Towne Lake, plus abundant shopping.</p>
<p><strong>Schools:</strong> Cypress-Fairbanks ISD (Cy-Fair ISD) serves most of the area.</p>
<p><strong>Commute:</strong> US-290 runs southeast toward the Loop, and the Grand Parkway and Beltway 8 link to Katy and the Energy Corridor.</p>
<p><strong>Flood considerations:</strong> Cypress Creek and its tributaries cross the area, and some neighborhoods along them have flooded in past storms. Check the parcel and its history.</p>
<p><strong>What to check:</strong> Because Cypress is unincorporated, services often come from MUDs and the county, so compare MUD rates and HOA rules carefully.</p>

<h2>Friendswood</h2>
<p><strong>Vibe:</strong> A smaller, tight-knit city between Houston and Galveston, with a quieter, small-town feel and easy access to Clear Lake.</p>
<p><strong>Schools:</strong> Friendswood ISD serves much of the city, and parts are zoned to Clear Creek ISD.</p>
<p><strong>Commute:</strong> I-45 and SH 288 are the main routes north. NASA's Johnson Space Center and the Clear Lake employers are close.</p>
<p><strong>Flood considerations:</strong> Clear Creek runs through Friendswood. During Harvey, local police reported flood damage to more than 3,000 homes in the city. Ask detailed questions about past flooding, elevation and any mitigation work.</p>
<p><strong>What to check:</strong> Flood history, whether the home has been raised or repaired, and flood insurance cost before you make an offer.</p>

<h2>League City</h2>
<p><strong>Vibe:</strong> A growing Galveston County city near Clear Lake and the bay, popular with families who want water access, newer subdivisions and proximity to the coast.</p>
<p><strong>Schools:</strong> Clear Creek ISD serves most of League City, with some areas zoned to Dickinson ISD.</p>
<p><strong>Commute:</strong> I-45 runs north to Houston and south to Galveston. The Clear Lake area job base keeps some commutes short.</p>
<p><strong>Flood considerations:</strong> Clear Creek and Dickinson Bayou both affect the area, and its closeness to Galveston Bay raises hurricane and storm surge concerns. Check the evacuation zone as well as the flood zone.</p>
<p><strong>What to check:</strong> Windstorm and flood insurance quotes, home elevation and the hurricane evacuation route.</p>

<h2>Kingwood</h2>
<p><strong>Vibe:</strong> Known as "The Livable Forest," Kingwood is a heavily wooded, established community with greenbelt trails. Houston annexed it in 1996, so it is inside the City of Houston.</p>
<p><strong>Schools:</strong> Most of Kingwood is in Humble ISD despite being inside Houston city limits. A small Montgomery County portion is in New Caney ISD.</p>
<p><strong>Commute:</strong> US-59/I-69 runs southwest toward downtown, and Bush Intercontinental Airport is relatively close.</p>
<p><strong>Flood considerations:</strong> Kingwood sits along the San Jacinto River and Lake Houston. Hundreds of homes flooded during Harvey, and Kingwood High School took on water. Street-level history matters a great deal here.</p>
<p><strong>What to check:</strong> The home's Harvey history, distance and elevation relative to the river, and flood insurance cost.</p>

<h2>Tomball and Fulshear</h2>
<p><strong>Vibe:</strong> Two edges of the metro for families who want more space. Tomball, to the northwest, has a historic downtown and a semi-rural feel. Fulshear, west of Katy in Fort Bend County, is a fast-growing area of large master-planned communities.</p>
<p><strong>Schools:</strong> Tomball ISD serves Tomball and much of the surrounding area, though nearby addresses can fall in other districts. Fulshear is split between Lamar CISD and Katy ISD, sometimes within the same master-planned community.</p>
<p><strong>Commute:</strong> Tomball uses SH 249 (the Tomball Parkway) and the Grand Parkway. Fulshear relies on the Westpark Tollway, I-10 and the Grand Parkway. Both are long drives to downtown at rush hour.</p>
<p><strong>Flood considerations:</strong> New construction is common, so ask about the subdivision's drainage design and detention ponds and look up the parcel on the FEMA map.</p>
<p><strong>What to check:</strong> School district by address, MUD rates on new sections, and future road construction near your lot.</p>

